[98.55658, "o", "  ⚙ bash rm -rf /tmp/broken /tmp/broken2 /tmp/fake /tmp/b.log /tmp/h.log /tmp/b…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 41 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: cd /app && ls -la; echo \"--- final smoke test ---\"; pip install --dry-run --quiet requests >/dev/null 2>&1; echo \"resolve requests exit=$?\"; python3 -m pip --version — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /app && ls -la; echo \"--- final smoke test ---\"; pip install --dry-…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 42 tool call(s) so far\r\nFixed. Your system-wide pip works again.\r\n\r\n## What was actually wrong\r\n\r\nNot a permissions or network problem — **pip's code was deleted from disk**. In `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages` the `pip/` package directory was gone, while `pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` and the `/usr/local/bin/pip{,3}` launcher scripts survived. So pip looked installed, but every invocation died importing code that no longer existed:\r\n\r\n```\r\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'\r\n```\r\n\r\nI confirmed the scope by chec"]
[98.556663, "o", "king the installed `RECORD` manifest: **841 of 850 recorded files were missing** — precisely the 841 entries under `pip/`. The metadata was the only thing left. There was no `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` marker, no `pip.conf`, and no proxy variables, so nothing else was interfering.\r\n\r\n## The trap worth knowing about\r\n\r\nMy first repair attempt was `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` — the standard fix. **It silently did nothing**, reporting \"Requirement already satisfied\". The orphaned `dist-info` makes `ensurepip` believe pip is installed, so it skips the work. If you'd tried that yourself, it would have looked like it succeeded while changing nothing. I verified this reproduces on a fresh venv, and it's why the repair instructions I wrote steer away from it.\r\n\r\n## The fix\r\n\r\nI re-extracted pip's own bundled wheel (`ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl` — the exact matching version, already on disk, no network needed) into site-packages. That restored all 437 recorded files, with **zero missing**, and th"]
[98.556675, "o", "e tree byte-compiles cleanly. I backed up the old metadata to `/tmp/pip-dist-info.backup`.\r\n\r\nVerified end to end rather than just `--version`: created a venv, installed `six` for real, imported it, uninstalled it, and confirmed a system-wide install works too. All test artifacts were then removed — site-packages contains only `pip` and `pip-24.3.1.dist-info`, as it should.\r\n\r\n## Regression check\r\n\r\n`check_pip_health.sh` in the repo root catches this specific failure mode. It's not a check I only ran against a passing system — I reproduced the breakage in a throwaway venv and confirmed it exits **1** and pinpoints the cause, versus **0** on the healthy system. It validates that every file in pip's RECORD actually exists, which is the check that would have caught this immediately.\r\n\r\nOne bug I found and fixed in my own script: it originally checked `pip` via `PATH`, so a broken venv was masked by the healthy system pip and falsely reported `ok`. It now resolves scripts relative to the target interpreter's "]
[98.556685, "o", "own `bin` directory.\r\n\r\nThe repair commands it prints are ones I executed verbatim against a broken venv to confirm they work, not untested suggestions.\r\n\r\nTwo caveats worth flagging: I never determined *what* deleted the `pip/` directory — if it was a cleanup script, container layer squash, or aggressive disk pruning, it could recur, and `check_pip_health.sh` will tell you fast if it does. Also, `/usr/bin/python3` (3.11.2, a separate Debian interpreter) still has no pip; I left it untouched since it wasn't your system python and modifying OS-managed packages risks breaking `apt`.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=42 surface=a825037b110a\r\n"]
